(* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *)
(*  FOSSIL — Grammar (EBNF) — v0.2                                     *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*  THIS FILE SPECIFIES THE LANGUAGE. The parser implements it; it     *)
(*  does not define it. A production written here and absent from      *)
(*  `crates/fossil-syntax` is WORK OUTSTANDING, not an error in this   *)
(*  file. That inversion is deliberate and it is the whole reason the  *)
(*  file exists apart from the parser: v0.1 of this document described *)
(*  what the parser happened to accept, which made every disagreement  *)
(*  a bug in the document by construction, and so the document could   *)
(*  never ask the parser for anything.                                 *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*  The surface below is the one decided by three rulings. They are    *)
(*  STATED here and not cited, because a citation outlives the file it *)
(*  points at and then says nothing:                                   *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*    THE IDENTITY IS A SLOT OF THE LANGUAGE. It is written as an      *)
(*    assignment, `@subject = <expr>`, on the first line of a mapping  *)
(*    body, and it is UNIQUE PER TYPE: every mapping that produces T   *)
(*    declares the same one, and two that disagree are an error naming *)
(*    both. It is not an attribute argument, and the reason it is not  *)
(*    is that no reviewed language admits a per-row expression in one. *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*    `.` MEANS «MEMBER OF», AND WHAT IS ON THE LEFT DECIDES WHAT THE  *)
(*    MEMBERS ARE. It is the only access operator: a namespace gives   *)
(*    its functions, a type its fields, a value its type's operations, *)
(*    a relation its verbs. The verbs are CATALOGUE ROWS reached       *)
(*    through it and not productions, so a new one is a row and never  *)
(*    a rule.                                                          *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*    `|>` DIES, and the member call is the spelling. It is the        *)
(*    ruling of 2026-08-11 that reaches this file hardest. The two     *)
(*    others that reach a production — naming a shape document is      *)
(*    mandatory, and the user declares no functions — are stated at    *)
(*    the production they bind. All of them are recorded, with their   *)
(*    arguments, in SURFACE-PLAN.md.                                   *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*  THE CONFORMANCE SET is `apps/docs/programs/` — 23 programs with    *)
(*  their shape documents and data, 5 of which are expected to fail    *)
(*  the checker and carry the diagnostic they must produce. Every one  *)
(*  of the 23 is derivable from the productions below, and no          *)
(*  production below exists for a form none of them spells, except     *)
(*  where a comment says so and says why. Nothing mechanically checks  *)
(*  this file against the parser today, so those 23 programs are the   *)
(*  only check it has.                                                 *)
(*                                                                     *)
(*  What a well-formed program MEANS, as against how it is spelled —   *)
(*  the judgments, the subtyping relation, and the exact statement of  *)
(*  what a successful compile promises — is `/docs/book/typing`. It    *)
(*  stands to the checker as this file stands to the parser, and the   *)
(*  two are the only normative documents about the language.           *)
(* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *)


(* ═══ LEXICAL LAYER ═══ *)
(* Tokens produced by the lexer. Whitespace is significant            *)
(* (indentation tokens INDENT/DEDENT). Comments are discarded.        *)

LETTER         := [a-zA-Z]
DIGIT          := [0-9]
SPACE          := ' '
TAB            := '\t'
WHITESPACE     := SPACE | TAB
NEWLINE        := '\n'

(* INDENT and DEDENT are virtual tokens emitted by the lexer          *)
(* based on column tracking. Spaces or tabs but consistent per block. *)
(*                                                                    *)
(* A line that carries only a comment, and a blank line, DO NOT touch *)
(* the indentation stack, whatever column they start at. This is not  *)
(* a nicety: the conformance programs mark their transcluded regions  *)
(* with `// #endregion name` at column 0 in the middle of an indented *)
(* mapping body, and a comment that closed the block would make the   *)
(* documentation unable to quote the program it documents.            *)
INDENT, DEDENT := computed

(* Identifiers and keywords *)
IDENT          := (LETTER | '_') (LETTER | DIGIT | '_')*
KEYWORD        := 'from' | 'and' | 'or' | 'not'

(* `prefix` was a keyword and is gone with the CURIE: a program writes full
   IRIs inside interpolated strings and short names everywhere else, so there
   is no vocabulary declaration left to introduce.

   `iri` was a keyword and is gone with `@subject(iri = …)`: the identity is an
   assignment now, so the word named an argument that no production takes. It
   was also a plausible column name held hostage.

   `where`, `select`, `join` and `on` are NOT keywords and must never become
   any. They are catalogue entries and named arguments — ordinary identifiers
   that the checker resolves against a receiver type. See the tombstone under
   POSTFIX for the productions they used to have. *)

(* Boolean literals. `true` and `false` are tokens, not identifiers: a program
   writes `verified = true` and there is no binding for the name to resolve
   against, so leaving them as IDENT makes a literal look like an unresolved
   reference. *)
BOOL           := 'true' | 'false'

(* Numeric literals — decimal only, optional underscores as separators *)
INTEGER        := DIGIT (DIGIT | '_')*
FLOAT          := INTEGER '.' INTEGER

(* String literals. Interpolation is ALWAYS ACTIVE and `{{` is the escape for a
   literal '{'. A closing '}' needs no escape: outside a hole it has only one
   reading.

   A string with no hole is one STRING token. One WITH a hole is carved by the
   post-lexer pass into the run below, so the hole's expression is read by the
   ordinary expression parser — the interpolation is IN THIS GRAMMAR, not in the
   lexer, which is the lesson PEP 701 paid seven years for.

   This is also where a full IRI is written, now that there is no other place
   to write one: `"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name"`, `"https://shop.example/
   user/{User.email}"`. *)
STRING         := '"' STRING_CONTENT* '"'
STRING_CONTENT := REGULAR_CHAR | ESCAPE | '{{'
REGULAR_CHAR   := any char except '"', '\', '{' and NEWLINE
ESCAPE         := '\n' | '\t' | '\r' | '\\' | '\"'

STRING_OPEN    := '"'
STRING_TEXT    := (REGULAR_CHAR | ESCAPE | '{{')*
INTERP_OPEN    := '{'
STRING_CLOSE   := '"'

(* There is no TEMPLATE. The backtick literal and its TPL_CONTENT / TPL_ESCAPE
   run were a second spelling of the string above, differing only in the
   delimiter and in `${` for the hole — two ways to say one thing, which is the
   one rule this repo does not bend. `"…{expr}…"` is the spelling; `${`, '`'
   and `\$` are not tokens. *)

(* There is no ABS_IRI. `<http://…>` had exactly two consumers — the vocabulary
   declaration and the property key — and both are bare names now. A constant
   IRI is a STRING, and the shape decides that it denotes rather than reads:
   the type decides what there is. The lexical win is that `<` and
   `>` have one reading each and the lexer no longer has to guess between a
   comparison and the start of an IRI. *)

(* Comments — discarded by lexer. `// #region name` / `// #endregion name` are
   ordinary comments; the docs transclude program fragments by those markers,
   which is why 17 of the 23 conformance programs carry them. *)
COMMENT        := '//' (any char except NEWLINE)*

(* Operators *)
DEFINE         := ':='                (* binds a name *)
ASSIGN         := '='                 (* assigns a value *)
SHAPE_SEP      := ':'
T_QUESTION     := '?'

EQ             := '=='
NEQ            := '!='
LT             := '<'
LE             := '<='
GT             := '>'
GE             := '>='

PLUS           := '+'
MINUS          := '-'
STAR           := '*'
SLASH          := '/'
PERCENT        := '%'

DOT            := '.'
COMMA          := ','
LPAREN, RPAREN := '(' ')'
LBRACE, RBRACE := '{' '}'

(* There is no PIPE. `a |> f()` was a second spelling of `a.f()` and the ruling
   of 2026-08-11 kills it: the member call is the spelling, and with member
   resolution by receiver type the pipeline had nothing left that the dot could
   not do. `|>` is not a token. *)

(* Attributes. `@` is the language's own namespace, against the bare names that
   the shape document declares — the criterion is Django's («model metadata is
   anything that's not a field») with JSON-LD's solution, which reserves
   `@`+ALPHA for the same reason: a user's keys are arbitrary and you cannot
   appropriate `id`.

   Two names are accepted, by two productions, and the position tells them
   apart: `@rename` above a type binding, `@subject` as the first line of a
   mapping body. Any other name is an error. *)
AT_ATTR        := '@' IDENT


(* ═══ GRAMMAR ═══ *)

(* ─── Top level ─── *)

Program             := TopLevel* EOF

TopLevel            := SourceDef
                     | MultiSourceDef
                     | TypeDef
                     | Mapping

(* `@rename` is NOT a fifth alternative, and looking for one here is the
   mistake this note exists to stop. `TypeDef := RenameAttr* 'type' …` below,
   so `@rename(…) type { … } := …` already derives from `TopLevel` through
   `TypeDef`. What the `RenameAttr*` prefix changed is the FIRST set —
   `TypeDef` now starts with `AT_ATTR` as well as with the contextual `type` —
   which is a fact about the parser's dispatch and not about this production.

   LL(1) survives it. `AT_ATTR` has exactly one derivation at top level:
   `SubjectAssign`, the other thing that lexes as one, sits inside a
   `MappingBody`, which is reachable only through an `INDENT`. That is
   disambiguation rule 6 — one token, two names, POSITION decides — and it is
   why neither name needs a token of its own. *)

(* There is no PrefixDecl. `prefix ex: <http://example.org/>` introduced the
   CURIE, and the CURIE is gone from every position it held: a shape name, a
   property key, an expression and an interpolation hole are all bare names or
   full IRIs in strings now. A vocabulary declaration that nothing spells is a
   statement about nothing.

   There is no import production either. `use foo/bar { a, b } as c` parsed
   into IMPORT / IMPORT_PATH / SELECTIVE_IMPORT / ALIAS and nothing below the
   parser read any of them: a file is compiled alone, and there is no module
   system for a name to come from. `use` is an ordinary identifier. *)


(* ─── Bindings ─── *)

(* `:=` binds a name; `=` assigns a value. One binder, and what is being bound
   is read off the left-hand side. Every `=` in this grammar is an assignment:
   a body property, `@subject`, and a named argument. *)

(* A source binding binds BOTH the type and the relation under one name:
   `User := io.csv("data/users.csv")` makes `User.age` a field and `from User`
   a stream of rows. F# separates `CsvProvider<…>` from `.Load(…)` because
   there a type is reused across files; here a binding IS one concrete source,
   so separating them would be ceremony with no work in it. The consequence
   for names is that a mapping body writes `User.name` and
   never `Adults.name`, even when it draws `from Adults`. *)
SourceDef           := IDENT DEFINE Expression

(* Destructuring source: one binding per shape declared by the source schema.
   Binding is POSITIONAL — the Nth name binds the Nth shape the document
   declares, and the name is a free local label.

   No conformance program spells one: every source in the 23 is single-shape
   (`io.csv`, `io.json`). It stays because the type side below is the same
   production in type position and the two must not drift apart. *)
MultiSourceDef      := LBRACE IDENT (COMMA IDENT)* RBRACE DEFINE Expression

(* Type binding: the same destructuring, in type position. `type` is NOT a
   reserved word — at top level `IDENT LBRACE` is free, and the next token
   separates this from `SourceDef` (`:=` directly after the name) and from a
   mapping header (`:`). That matters: `type` is a plausible column name in a
   language whose commonest predicate is `rdf:type`.

   Naming a shape document is MANDATORY (the ruling of 2026-08-11). A bare
   property key takes its name from a predicate that a shape declares, so
   without a shape you cannot write a single property, and a program with no
   `type` binding has no mapping. *)
TypeDef             := RenameAttr* 'type'
                       LBRACE IDENT (COMMA IDENT)* RBRACE DEFINE Expression

(* The short name of a property is the last segment of the predicate IRI that
   the shape declares. Two predicates whose last segments coincide are an
   ERROR naming both IRIs — never disambiguated by the compiler, because the
   alternative is measured: FSharp.Data's numeric-suffix scheme and its
   singulariser produced `Purchasis` and renamed members across a minor
   version, breaking a program in production (PLDI 2016 §6.5). The repair is
   the author's, it is all constants, and it sits above the declaration in the
   shape of Prisma's `@map`:

       @rename(Person, "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name" as foaf_name)
       type { Person } := io.shex("mixed.shex")

   It goes in the program and not in the `.shex` because the vocabulary may
   not be yours. AT_ATTR here must be `@rename`; the IDENT is one of the names
   this binding introduces. *)
RenameAttr          := AT_ATTR LPAREN IDENT (COMMA Rename)+ RPAREN
Rename              := STRING 'as' IDENT

(* `as` is one of two survivors of the word (the other is AliasArg, for the
   self-join). It is CONTEXTUAL, not reserved: it is recognised only between
   two operands where no expression could continue, and everywhere else it is
   an ordinary identifier. See RESERVED KEYWORDS. *)


(* ─── Mappings ─── *)

Mapping             := MappingHeader NEWLINE INDENT MappingBody DEDENT

(* `Users : Person from Adults`. Two names, because two sources can produce
   `Person` and a diagnostic has to be able to say which. Since
   the mapping keeps its name, the trailing `:` that would have opened the
   block is redundant — indentation opens it, and two `:` in one header are
   noise. *)
MappingHeader       := IDENT SHAPE_SEP ShapeExpr 'from' Expression

(* The shape is a NAME — one of the names a `type { … }` binding introduced.
   It was `IRIExpr` (a CURIE, an absolute IRI or a template), and it is a name
   now because there is nothing left for the other two to be written against:
   the vocabulary declaration a CURIE expanded through is gone, and the shape
   document already names every shape it declares. A header that spelled the
   IRI would name, a second time and in a second syntax, the very thing the
   `type { … }` binding above it bound.

   One shape, not an intersection. `A & B` parsed and then lowered to `A`
   alone, silently, because the lowering took the first IRI and dropped the
   rest — a mapping that type-checked against half of what it declared. The
   node stays because the header needs somewhere to put its one shape; `&` is
   not a token.

   There is no `in g` either. The named graph clause parsed into an IN_CLAUSE
   nothing read, and the corpus has nowhere to put a graph name: GraphAr is
   vertex and edge tables, not quads. `in` is an ordinary identifier. *)
ShapeExpr           := IDENT

(* `from` takes a full expression, and that is where the verbs live: `from
   Adults`, `from User.where(User.age >= 18)`, `from Purchase.join(User, on =
   Purchase.user_id == User.id)`. All three are the same production. *)

MappingBody         := SubjectAssign Property+

(* The identity: an ASSIGNMENT, required, exactly one, and the first line of
   the body. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, read by the
   ordinary expression parser, which is the point — `@subject(iri = …)`
   had to be argued each time to be «syntax wearing a sigil» in order to
   survive its own attribute law, and no reviewed language admits a per-row
   expression in an attribute argument.

   It is the first line of the BODY and not a line above the header because
   the row binder is introduced BY the header: above it, `User` does not exist
   yet.

   AT_ATTR here must be `@subject`. Two obligations that no other property
   carries, both checker-enforced and neither expressible here: the expression
   is deterministic and bounded to the row, and every mapping producing the
   same type declares the SAME `@subject` — otherwise error, naming both
   mappings and both templates (the `errors/two-identities` conformance
   program is the one that must produce that diagnostic). *)
SubjectAssign       := AT_ATTR ASSIGN Expression

Property            := PropertyLhs ASSIGN Expression

(* A bare name. `name = User.name`, never `ex:name = …` and never
   `<http://…/name> = …`. The name is the last segment of the predicate IRI
   that the shape declares — which is what `fossil-mir` already computed
   internally, promoted to being what the author writes. *)
PropertyLhs         := IDENT

(* There is no annotation block. `p = e { a = v }` was a statement about a
   statement, and saying one needs a term that names the base triple —
   RDF-star's, which left this grammar with the triple term. Nothing below the
   parser ever read an ANNOTATION_BLOCK. It comes back when there is something
   to reify with. *)


(* ─── Expressions (precedence-ordered, low binding → high) ─── *)

Expression          := TernaryExpr

(* There is no PipelineExpr. See the PIPE tombstone in the lexical layer:
   `a |> f()` and `a.f()` were one idea with two spellings, and the dot won. *)

(* L1 — Ternary, right-assoc. The `:` is SHAPE_SEP — the same token the mapping
   header takes. There is no T_COLON: it was declared as a terminal of its own
   and nothing ever produced one, so the grammar named a token the lexer does
   not emit. What separates the two readings is the node, not the lexeme. *)
TernaryExpr         := OrExpr (T_QUESTION OrExpr SHAPE_SEP TernaryExpr)?

(* L2 — Or *)
OrExpr              := AndExpr ('or' AndExpr)*

(* L3 — And *)
AndExpr             := CompExpr ('and' CompExpr)*

(* L4 — Comparison, non-associative *)
CompExpr            := AdditiveExpr (CompOp AdditiveExpr)?
CompOp              := EQ | NEQ | LT | LE | GT | GE

(* L5 — Additive *)
AdditiveExpr        := MulExpr ((PLUS | MINUS) MulExpr)*

(* L6 — Multiplicative *)
MulExpr             := UnaryExpr ((STAR | SLASH | PERCENT) UnaryExpr)*

(* L7 — Unary *)
UnaryExpr           := MINUS UnaryExpr
                     | 'not' UnaryExpr
                     | PostfixExpr

(* L8 — Postfix (member access, call), tightest.

   `.` is the ONLY access operator and it means «member of». What is on the
   left decides what the members are:

     a namespace   `io`, `parse`, `validate`, `math`, `anon`
                   → its functions.        `parse.float(Row.celsius)`
     a type        `User`, `Person`, `str`, `seq`
                   → its fields, or its operations reached through the type.
                                           `User.email`, `str.trim(x)`
     a value       any expression
                   → its type's operations. `User.email.trim().lower()`
     a relation    `Users`, `Adults`, `User`
                   → its operations.       `User.where(User.age >= 18)`

   There is no `::`. Rust's split between a compile-time path and a run-time
   access has no referent here: a fossil program executes nothing, and
   `User.age` is a column reference that ends up as SQL. Importing two
   operators would import a distinction that does not exist.

   THE VERBS ARE NOT IN THIS GRAMMAR, and their absence is the change.
   `where`, `select` and `join` had productions of their own; they are
   catalogue entries now, resolved by receiver type, and a new verb is a ROW
   rather than a RULE. There is deliberately no generic `Verb` production to
   put them back — that would smuggle in exactly what was removed. What
   remains is `PostfixOp`: a member, and a call.

   `str.lower(str.trim(x))` and `x.trim().lower()` are ONE catalogue entry
   reached two ways, not two ideas — `s.len()` ≡ `str::len(&s)` in Rust,
   `s.upper()` ≡ `str.upper(s)` in Python. The cost, accepted knowingly when
   the rule was taken, is that the corpus contains both and it has to be
   explained once; the `expressions` and `contacts` programs each spell both
   on purpose.

   AN EDGE IS A CALL. `buyer = Person(User.email)` is the destination type
   applied to an expression — «the Person whose identity is built from this
   email». It needs no production of its own: a type name is a PrimaryExpr and
   the application is the ordinary call below. The lowering knows the type and
   uses THE template for it — there is exactly one, because the identity is
   unique per type, and that uniqueness is what retires `subject_skeletons`
   and its `\u{1}` marker: an edge used to be inferred by comparing template
   skeletons between mappings, which needed the marker and could match where
   it must not. *)
PostfixExpr         := PrimaryExpr (PostfixOp)*
PostfixOp           := DOT IDENT
                     | LPAREN ArgList? RPAREN

ArgList             := Arg (COMMA Arg)*
Arg                 := NamedArg | AliasArg | Expression
NamedArg            := IDENT ASSIGN Expression

(* The self-join, and the only other place `as` survives: `Node.join(Node as
   Other, on = Node.parent == Other.id)`. The alias binds a second name for
   the same source so the two sides of the join can be told apart, and the
   mapping body then writes `Other.label` next to `Node.label`. *)
AliasArg            := IDENT 'as' IDENT

(* Primary expressions *)
PrimaryExpr         := Literal
                     | IDENT
                     | LPAREN Expression RPAREN

(* There is no FieldRef. `.name` was a reference to a column of an anonymous
   current row, and the row has a name now: every reference is qualified,
   `User.name`. It took three pieces of machinery with it —
   `expr_contains_free_field_refs`, `rewrite_field_refs_to_row_dot` and
   `synthesize_closure` existed ONLY because a predicate did not name its row.
   A leading `.` is an error. *)

(* There is no IRIExpr, no PrefixedName and no LocalName. `ex:name` was the
   third thing that looked like member access and was not (`str.slug`,
   `users.name`, `ex:name` — three spellings, one of them not a member). The
   two that WERE member access survive as one rule, and `ex:name` dies with
   the CURIE: what is on the left decides the members, and a prefix is not
   something a member can be taken of. A `:` that is not a mapping header or
   a ternary is an error. *)


(* ─── Literals ─── *)

Literal             := INTEGER | FLOAT | STRING | BOOL
                     | InterpolatedString

(* A string with at least one hole. One spelling, one node. *)
InterpolatedString  := STRING_OPEN (STRING_TEXT | Interpolation)* STRING_CLOSE
Interpolation       := INTERP_OPEN Expression RBRACE

(* The hole takes an EXPRESSION and nothing else. There is no PrefixRef:
   `${ex:}` — a prefix with no local part — was the CURIE-with-holes, admitted
   in this one position and nowhere else, and it died with the CURIE. A full
   IRI is written out: `"https://shop.example/user/{User.email}"`. *)


(* ─── Record literals (blank node value position) ─── *)

(* There is no record literal. `{ k = v }` in value position was the blank node,
   and a blank node is a vertex with no IRI — which GraphAr's vertex table has no
   row shape for and `MappingBody` has no way to declare. It went with the
   annotation block it shared a `{` with: the one disambiguation rule this
   grammar had existed only to tell the two apart, and with neither of them
   there is nothing to tell apart. *)


(* ═══ DISAMBIGUATION RULES (parser context) ═══ *)

(*  (There used to be a rule 1 here: RecordLiteral vs AnnotationBlock, on  *)
(*   whether the `{` came straight after `=` or after an expression. Both  *)
(*   forms are gone, and a rule that told two absent things apart is not   *)
(*   a rule. A `{` in a mapping body is now an error, and only one.)       *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  (There used to be a rule 2 here: DOT IDENT vs FieldRef, on whether an  *)
(*   expression preceded the dot. FieldRef is gone; a leading `.` starts   *)
(*   nothing.)                                                             *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  3. `:` in a header vs `:` in a ternary                                 *)
(*     - Both are SHAPE_SEP; the lexer emits one token and only one.       *)
(*     - `Name : Shape from …`  → consumed by MappingHeader                *)
(*     - `cond ? a : b`         → consumed by TernaryExpr, paired with `?` *)
(*     The old third reading — `ex:name`, a PrefixedName, which needed a   *)
(*     no-whitespace-before-the-colon rule to keep `a : b` out of a        *)
(*     ternary — is gone with the CURIE. The contiguity check goes with    *)
(*     it.                                                                 *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  (There used to be a rule 4 here: `<<` vs `<`. The triple term went     *)
(*   with the RDF-specific surface, so `<<` claims nothing and `<` has     *)
(*   only ever one reading. With ABS_IRI gone, `<` no longer even opens.)  *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  5. `type` at top level                                                 *)
(*     - `type {` → TypeDef.  `type :=` → SourceDef binding the name       *)
(*       `type`.  `type :` → a mapping called `type`.  One token of        *)
(*       lookahead, and the word stays available as a column name.         *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  6. `@rename` vs `@subject`                                             *)
(*     - Both lex as AT_ATTR; position decides. At top level, immediately  *)
(*       above a `type` binding → RenameAttr. As the first line inside a   *)
(*       mapping body → SubjectAssign. Neither name is valid in the        *)
(*       other's position, and no third name is valid anywhere.            *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  7. `as`                                                                *)
(*     - Contextual, in exactly two places: `STRING as IDENT` inside       *)
(*       `@rename`, and `IDENT as IDENT` as an argument. In both, an       *)
(*       operand is followed by a bare IDENT, which no expression can      *)
(*       continue, so one token of lookahead settles it and `as` stays an  *)
(*       ordinary identifier everywhere else.                              *)


(* ═══ RESERVED KEYWORDS (cannot be identifiers) ═══ *)

(*  from     and      or       not      true     false                    *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  CONTEXTUAL, and therefore still usable as names: `type` (rule 5) and   *)
(*  `as` (rule 7).                                                         *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  NOT KEYWORDS, and this is load-bearing: `where`, `select`, `join`,     *)
(*  `on`, `io`, `str`, `seq`, `parse`, `validate`, `math`, `anon`. Every   *)
(*  one is an ordinary identifier that the checker resolves against a      *)
(*  catalogue by receiver type. Reserving any of them would undo in the    *)
(*  lexer the rule the grammar was rewritten around — the verbs stopped    *)
(*  being grammar, and a keyword is grammar.                               *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  `prefix` and `iri` were reserved and are not. `in`, `use` and `as`     *)
(*  were reserved for the named-graph clause and the import, and both      *)
(*  went; `as` came back contextual for the self-join and `@rename`. A     *)
(*  word reserved for a form the language does not have costs every        *)
(*  program that wanted it as a column name.                               *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  The `@` sigil IS decided, and against `#`: `#` is unlexable here, is   *)
(*  Turtle's comment character, and is RDF's fragment separator. `@` is    *)
(*  shared with keasy's connection aliases, which live inside string       *)
(*  literals, so the two never cross lexically.                            *)


(* ═══ OPERATOR PRECEDENCE TABLE ═══ *)

(*  Level   Operators                    Associativity                    *)
(*  ─────   ─────────────────────────    ─────────────                    *)
(*   L1     ? :                           right-assoc                      *)
(*   L2     or                            left-assoc                       *)
(*   L3     and                           left-assoc                       *)
(*   L4     == != < <= > >=               non-assoc                        *)
(*   L5     + -                           left-assoc                       *)
(*   L6     * / %                         left-assoc                       *)
(*   L7     - (unary), not                right-assoc                      *)
(*   L8     . (member), ( ) (call)        left-assoc                       *)
(*                                                                         *)
(*  Levels renumbered: `|>` held L1 and is gone, so everything moved up    *)
(*  one. A citation of «L8 unary» or «L9 postfix» predates this table.     *)


(* ═══ NOT IN MVP (productions intentionally absent) ═══ *)

(*  LambdaExpr        — no user-defined multi-arg lambdas                  *)
(*  MatchExpr         — no pattern matching (use multiple mappings)        *)
(*  IfThenElseExpr    — only via ternary `?:`                              *)
(*  ExternDecl        — no `extern` declarations in MVP                    *)
(*  WhileLoop/ForLoop — no imperative control flow                         *)
(*  MutableBinding    — no mutation                                        *)
(*  FunctionDecl      — the user declares no functions. Polymorphism lives *)
(*                      in the library only (ruling 2 of 2026-08-11)       *)
(*  TypeAnnotation    — no `f :: T -> U` over a value; nothing reads one   *)
(*  ExportedDefinition— no `@export`; there is no module system to export  *)
(*                      to, so the marker named nobody                     *)
(*  PartialPlaceholder— no `f(_, x)`; `_` is an ordinary identifier        *)
(*  Type parameters   — no generics                                        *)
(*  `@graph`          — the quad's named graph enters by the same door as  *)
(*                      `@subject` when the corpus schema has a place to   *)
(*                      put one. It does not today, so there is no slot.   *)
(*  Destination syntax— `io` names only the INPUT. Where a program writes  *)
(*                      to has no syntax at all, and inventing one here    *)
(*                      before it is decided is how v0.1 got its ghosts.   *)


(* ═══ OPEN — decided nowhere, and a grammar is where that shows ═══ *)

(*  These are not «not in MVP»: they are forms the language needs and no  *)
(*  record fixes. Each was hit while writing a production above.          *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  (1 was `@rename`'s repetition — whether it may appear twice over one  *)
(*   binding and whether one may carry several `STRING as IDENT`. DECIDED *)
(*   2026-08-12, and permissively: `RenameAttr*` with `(COMMA Rename)+`   *)
(*   is what the parser implements. The argument is that the alternative  *)
(*   is a LIMIT, and a limit needs a reason — one repair per binding is   *)
(*   not a property of anything, and a vocabulary that collides twice is  *)
(*   the same vocabulary that collided once. `+` and not `*` on `Rename`  *)
(*   is the one restriction kept: `@rename(Person)` renames nothing and   *)
(*   would be a line that looks like a repair and is not one.)            *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  2. Short destructuring — naming fewer members than the document       *)
(*     declares. `MultiSourceDef` and `TypeDef` are written as if the     *)
(*     count must match, because binding is positional; nothing says so.  *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  (3 was the edge constructor with more than one hole — the binding     *)
(*   order, and whether an argument is the hole's INPUT or its finished   *)
(*   value. DECIDED 2026-08-12 by the repo owner, and both halves the     *)
(*   same way: POSITIONAL in order of appearance, and each argument is    *)
(*   the hole's FINISHED VALUE. `Line(a, b)` against                      *)
(*   `"…/{LineRow.tenant}/line/{LineRow.id}"` puts `a` where the tenant   *)
(*   was and `b` where the id was, and the defining mapping's own         *)
(*   expressions are replaced rather than re-applied.                     *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*   Three reasons, and the last two are the ones that decide it:         *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*   - The language then has ONE binding rule. `type { A, B } := …` binds *)
(*     the Nth name to the Nth declared shape already; by-name binding    *)
(*     here would be a second idea.                                       *)
(*   - By-name would make the hole's SPELLING public API. The identifier  *)
(*     inside `{…}` is a column of the DEFINING mapping's row; renaming   *)
(*     it would break every call site that references the type.           *)
(*   - «Input» is not even well-defined across mappings. The hole's       *)
(*     expression is written in the defining mapping's scope, and that    *)
(*     row need not exist where the constructor is called — `Orders`      *)
(*     joins `User`, but a third mapping referencing `Person` need not.   *)
(*     Re-applying it would resolve a name the author never wrote.        *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*   Arity is checked and a mismatch names both counts and the mapping    *)
(*   that declared the identity. Zero holes is legal: a constant IRI is   *)
(*   an identity, and its constructor takes no arguments.)                *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  4. DECIDED 2026-08-14. `select` may follow a join, and it names a    *)
(*     QUALIFIED column: `Active.select(Employee.id, Order.total)`. The   *)
(*     surface already required this — the refusal reads «`select` takes  *)
(*     qualified column references and this is not one» — and the CST     *)
(*     already carries the binding. What did not carry it was the HIR:    *)
(*     `HirSourceOp::Select` held column names only, so a name was looked *)
(*     for in the rows in order and taken from the first that had it.     *)
(*     After a join that made `select(id)` mean «the left side's id» for  *)
(*     a reason the author never wrote. The payload carries the binding   *)
(*     now, so no production changes and the ambiguity has nowhere left   *)
(*     to live.                                                           *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  5. Whether a mapping may have a `@subject` and no properties.         *)
(*     `Property+` above says no, inherited from v0.1 rather than         *)
(*     decided; a shape that requires nothing is unwritable under it.     *)
(*                                                                        *)
(*  This file describes the language, not the parser's tolerance. A form  *)
(*  no downstream stage reads does not belong here — it is a promise the  *)
(*  language does not keep. The converse now also holds: a form the       *)
(*  language keeps belongs here whether or not the parser has caught up.  *)
